        <div id="test-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Test Your Well Before You Buy a Softener: Hardness, Iron and pH
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best water softener for well water is the one sized to your actual test results, and well owners have no utility report to fall back on. Test before you shop. A softener chosen on guesswork is either too small, too large, or fighting a problem it was never designed to fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hardness in Grains per Gallon</h3>

          <p>
            Softeners are sized in grains, so you need hardness in grains per gallon (gpg). Many test kits report it in parts per million or milligrams per liter instead; divide that figure by 17.1 to convert. Water above about 7 gpg is generally considered hard, and well water above 15 or 20 gpg is common in limestone areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Iron and Manganese in ppm</h3>

          <p>
            Iron is the number that changes the whole recommendation. Test total iron in ppm, and note whether water comes out of the tap clear and turns orange when it sits (dissolved ferrous iron) or comes out already tinted (ferric iron). Test manganese too, which behaves like iron and leaves black stains.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">pH, Sulfur and Bacteria</h3>

          <p>
            Low pH water is corrosive and can eat copper pipes, a rotten-egg smell signals hydrogen sulfide, and coliform bacteria need disinfection. None of those is a softener's job, but all of them change what goes in front of it. Our <a href="/best-water-test-kit/">best water test kits</a> guide covers home kits; a certified lab test is worth it the first time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="iron-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Iron Can a Water Softener Remove? Usually Only a Few ppm
          </h2>

          <p>
            Softeners remove some iron, and that is both their strength on wells and the most common cause of failure. Ion exchange resin will swap dissolved iron the same way it swaps calcium and magnesium, but only under the right conditions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear-Water (Ferrous) Iron: Yes, Within Limits</h3>

          <p>
            Dissolved ferrous iron, the kind that comes out clear, is what a softener can handle. A common rule among water treatment pros is that a standard softener manages a few ppm comfortably, and that higher levels shorten resin life. Fine mesh resin is made for this duty; Aquasure's iron model is the only pick here with a stated rating, at up to 10 ppm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Red-Water (Ferric) Iron and Iron Bacteria: No</h3>

          <p>
            Iron that has already oxidized, along with iron bacteria that leave orange slime in the toilet tank, will coat and clog a resin bed. A softener is not rated for either. These need an oxidizing iron filter, an air-injection system or chlorination ahead of the softener.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Put an Iron Filter First</h3>

          <p>
            If your test shows more than about 3 to 5 ppm of iron, any manganese above a trace, iron bacteria or ferric iron, put a dedicated iron filter before the softener. Our guide to the <a href="/best-water-filter-for-well-water/">best water filters for well water</a> covers iron and sediment systems. Treat iron and sediment first, then soften.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Size Water Softener Do I Need for Well Water? Do the Grain Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sizing takes four numbers and five minutes. Get it right and the softener regenerates about once a week, which keeps salt use reasonable and the resin fresh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Work Out Compensated Hardness</h3>

          <p>
            Iron uses up resin capacity, so add it to your hardness. A widely used rule adds about 4 grains for every 1 ppm of iron. A well with 18 gpg of hardness and 2 ppm of iron has a compensated hardness of about 26 gpg. Enter this figure, not the raw hardness, when sizing and when programming the valve.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Estimate Daily Water Use</h3>

          <p>
            Use about 75 gallons per person per day as a planning figure, or read your own meter if you have one. A family of four uses roughly 300 gallons a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Multiply for Daily Grains</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply compensated hardness by daily gallons. In our example, 26 gpg times 300 gallons is 7,800 grains a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Pick a Capacity for Weekly Regeneration</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply daily grains by seven for a week's load: 54,600 grains here. Rated capacities assume high salt settings, so leave headroom. That household should look at 64,000 grains rather than 48,000. A couple on the same water would need about 27,300 grains a week, which a 32,000 or 48,000-grain unit handles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Flow and Pipe Size Too</h3>

          <p>
            Capacity is half the decision. A softener also has a service flow rating, and well systems have little pressure to spare, so match the rating to your peak use. The table below shows each pick's key spec.
          </p>

