        <div id="pre-filter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Do You Need a Sediment Pre-Filter Before a Well Softener? Almost Always
          </h2>

          <p>
            Well water carries sand, silt and grit that city water does not, and a softener valve is a precision part. A sediment filter ahead of the softener is cheap insurance on almost every well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Sediment Does to a Softener</h3>

          <p>
            Grit scores the valve's seals and pistons, clogs the injector that draws brine, and fouls the meter, which is why DuraWater lists an upgraded paddle wheel meter on its iron model. A packed resin bed also loses flow and capacity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spin-Down or Cartridge Filter</h3>

          <p>
            A spin-down filter with a clear housing and a flush valve catches sand and lets you see and purge it in seconds, which suits sandy wells. A cartridge housing with a 5 to 20 micron sediment cartridge catches finer silt. Many wells benefit from both: spin-down first, then cartridge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Right Order of Equipment</h3>

          <p>
            Install treatment after the pressure tank in this order: sediment filter, iron or sulfur filter if needed, softener, then any UV disinfection or a drinking-water reverse osmosis unit. Sediment comes out first so it cannot reach anything else, and UV works best on water that is already clear and soft. Our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best water filtration systems for home</a> guide covers the wider system, and our <a href="/best-whole-house-water-filter/">whole house water filter</a> rankings compare flow rates and cartridge upkeep for the sediment and carbon stages.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="salt-vs-salt-free" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Salt vs Salt-Free Softeners on Well Water: Only Salt Actually Softens
          </h2>

          <p>
            Salt-free conditioners are popular because they need no salt, drain or electricity. On a well, they are rarely the right choice, and every pick in this guide is salt-based for that reason.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Salt-Based Ion Exchange</h3>

          <p>
            A salt-based softener swaps calcium, magnesium and some dissolved iron for sodium, then rinses the captured minerals away with brine. It removes hardness from the water, which you can confirm with a simple hardness test strip after installation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Salt-Free Conditioners</h3>

          <p>
            Template-assisted crystallization and similar conditioners change how hardness minerals behave so they form less scale, but the minerals stay in the water. Most are also sensitive to iron and manganese, which foul the media. That makes them a poor fit for typical well water. Our <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">best water softener systems</a> guide compares both types for city water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Potassium Chloride and Iron-Fighting Salt</h3>

          <p>
            If you want less sodium, most salt-based softeners can run on potassium chloride, though it costs more and may need a higher salt setting. On iron-bearing wells, salt pellets with an iron-fighting additive, such as Diamond Crystal Iron Fighter, help keep the resin clean between cleanings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Installing a Softener on a Well System: Where It Goes and What It Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A softener installs on the main line after the pressure tank. Most handy homeowners can do it with basic plumbing skills, but plan the location before the boxes arrive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Location and Bypass</h3>

          <p>
            Install it after the pressure tank and pressure switch, and after sediment and iron filters. Every pick here includes a bypass valve, which lets you send water around the softener for service. Many people also leave an outdoor spigot on unsoftened water, since there is no reason to soften water for the lawn.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drain, Air Gap and Power</h3>

          <p>
            Regeneration sends brine and backwash water to a drain, so you need a floor drain, standpipe or laundry sink nearby, with an air gap so wastewater cannot siphon back. The valve also needs a standard outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Well Pressure and Flow</h3>

          <p>
            Many well systems run around 40 to 60 psi. A softener sized too small for your flow adds pressure drop you will feel in the shower. That is why the service flow rating matters as much as grain capacity on a well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Septic Systems</h3>

          <p>
            Regeneration wastewater goes down the drain and, in most rural homes, into the septic system. A properly sized, metered softener that regenerates only when needed sends the least water and salt to the tank. Oversized or timer-based units send more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="settings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Setting a Metered Softener for Well Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            A metered valve is only as good as the numbers you program into it. Most poor-performing softeners on wells are set up for city water defaults.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Enter Compensated Hardness</h3>

          <p>
            Program the compensated hardness from your sizing math, not the raw hardness. If your water has 18 gpg and 2 ppm of iron, enter about 26. Entering 18 means the valve regenerates too late and iron starts to break through.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Regeneration Time and Reserve</h3>

          <p>
            Set regeneration for the middle of the night, typically 2 a.m., when nobody is drawing water. Metered valves hold back a reserve so soft water does not run out before regeneration; the default is usually fine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Backwash Length for Iron</h3>

          <p>
            Iron and sediment benefit from a thorough backwash. The Aquasure iron model lists a double backwash for this reason. On Fleck valves, a service tech or the seller's support line can lengthen backwash if iron stains reappear between cycles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Retest After Two Weeks</h3>

          <p>
            Test hardness at a cold tap two weeks after start-up. Soft water should read close to zero. If it does not, check the hardness setting, the salt level and the bypass position before suspecting the resin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Keeping a Well-Water Softener Healthy: Resin Cleaner, Salt and Bridges
          </h2>

          <p>
            A softener on a well needs a little more care than one on city water, mostly because of iron. The work is a few minutes a month.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use a Resin Cleaner on Iron-Bearing Water</h3>

          <p>
            Iron that is not fully rinsed out builds up on the resin and lowers capacity. Resin cleaners such as Pro Products Rust Out are added with the salt; its label calls for half a cup per 40 pounds of salt. Regular use keeps the bed exchanging properly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the Salt and Break Up Bridges</h3>

          <p>
            Keep the brine tank at least a third full. A hard crust, called a salt bridge, can form above the water and leave the softener regenerating with no brine. Push a broom handle down through the salt every couple of months to check.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Long Resin Lasts</h3>

          <p>
            Resin typically lasts many years, but iron, sediment and oxidizers shorten it. Ten percent crosslink resin, as used in several picks, is more durable than standard resin. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-do-water-softeners-last/">how long water softeners last</a> covers the valve, tanks and resin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smell-sodium" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Rotten-Egg Smell, Sodium and Other Well Softener Questions
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few problems show up on wells after a softener goes in. None is a reason not to soften, but each has a specific fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rotten-Egg Smell</h3>

          <p>
            A sulfur smell usually comes from hydrogen sulfide in the well or from bacteria, sometimes in the water heater. A softener will not remove it. Our guide to <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/">why water smells like rotten eggs</a> explains how to tell which you have.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sodium in Drinking Water</h3>

          <p>
            Softening adds a small amount of sodium, rising with hardness. If anyone in the house is on a low-sodium diet, use potassium chloride or put a reverse osmosis filter at the kitchen sink for drinking water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Orange Stains Coming Back</h3>

          <p>
            Stains after a softener is installed mean iron is breaking through. Check the compensated hardness setting, add resin cleaner, and retest iron. If iron is higher than the softener can handle, add an iron filter rather than turning up the salt. If you only want to stop scale and can live with hard water, a salt-free <a href="/best-water-conditioner/">water conditioner</a> is the alternative, and a certified <a href="/best-refrigerator-water-filter/">refrigerator water filter</a> still handles taste at the fridge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by Hardness and Ignoring Iron</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Iron uses up resin capacity just like hardness does. Sizing and programming a softener on raw hardness means it regenerates too late, iron breaks through, and orange stains return. Add about 4 grains per ppm of iron to get compensated hardness, and use that figure for both.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Softener to Fix Red Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water that comes out of the tap already orange contains ferric iron, and slimy orange deposits mean iron bacteria. A softener is not rated for either, and both will foul the resin. Treat them with a dedicated iron filter or oxidation system ahead of the softener.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Sediment Pre-Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sand and grit from a well wear the softener valve, clog the brine injector and foul the meter. A spin-down or cartridge sediment filter costs far less than a valve rebuild and makes every other piece of equipment last longer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Biggest Softener You Can Afford</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oversized softener regenerates rarely, so resin sits in hard, iron-bearing water for long stretches and fouls faster, and salt efficiency suffers. Size for regeneration about once a week using the grain math, not for the largest number on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Salt-Free for a Well</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Salt-free conditioners leave the hardness minerals in the water and are sensitive to iron and manganese, which most wells contain. If you want soft water on a well, choose a salt-based softener and, if sodium is a concern, run it on potassium chloride.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best water softener for well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most wells with hard water and some dissolved iron, the best water softener is a salt-based unit with fine mesh resin and a metered valve, such as the DuraWater Fleck 5600SXT Iron Blaster at 48,000 grains. If your test shows higher clear-water iron, the Aquasure Harmony iron model is rated for up to 10 ppm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a water softener if I have well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. Well water needs a softener only if it is hard, generally above about 7 grains per gallon, or if hardness is causing scale, spotting or dry skin. Test first: many wells need sediment, iron or bacteria treatment before softening, and some soft wells need no softener at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a water softener remove iron from well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A salt-based softener removes dissolved ferrous iron, the kind that comes out of the tap clear, within limits. Most handle a few ppm; fine mesh resin and iron-rated models handle more, with Aquasure's rated up to 10 ppm. Softeners are not rated for ferric iron or iron bacteria, which need a dedicated iron filter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size water softener do I need for a family of four on a well?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Multiply compensated hardness, which is hardness plus about 4 grains per ppm of iron, by about 300 gallons a day, then by seven. A family of four with 15 gpg and no iron needs about 31,500 grains a week, so a 48,000-grain softener fits. Very hard or iron-rich water may need 64,000 grains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a water softener go before or after the pressure tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                After the pressure tank. The pressure tank and switch sit right after the well pump, and all treatment goes downstream of them, in the order sediment filter, iron filter if needed, softener, then UV or reverse osmosis. Installing a softener before the pressure tank exposes it to pump surges and unregulated pressure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a salt-free water softener good for well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Salt-free conditioners do not remove hardness minerals, and most are sensitive to iron and manganese, which foul their media. Many manufacturers specify very low iron limits. On a well, a salt-based softener with a sediment pre-filter is the more reliable choice, and potassium chloride can replace sodium chloride if you want less sodium.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a water softener bad for a septic system?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly sized, metered softener is generally considered compatible with a septic system in good condition. It sends regeneration water and brine to the tank only when the resin needs it. Oversized or timer-based softeners send more water and salt. Check local rules, since a few areas restrict softener discharge to septic systems.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

